    "title": "Clone-isolation checks recognize only current clones of the same factory",
    "description": "Both creation-time validation and runtime destination screening consult only the current factory's isRaffle registry. A Raffle clone from another factory is absent from that registry while still reporting IERC721 support through inheritance. Concretely, factory B can accept existing clone A as a prize-token contract; transfer of an A ticket to the new B clone passes A's local destination screen, and B's expected-prize callback activates, moving an asset directly between raffle clones. A foreign clone can also be selected as sponsorRecipient or protocolTreasury, after which _transferQuote sends USDC into that clone as an unaccounted balance. The reviewed clone exposes no generic execute or rescue path for such USDC. These paths violate the absolute cross-clone isolation requirement and can strand assets, although they require a sponsor, deployer, or ticket owner to select or approve the foreign clone.\nAssumptions: Two independently deployed reviewed factories and their clones exist, and neither factory registers the other's clones.; A sponsor, factory deployer, or ticket owner deliberately selects or approves a foreign clone address; no external global registry reconciliation is assumed.; For the payout-lock consequence, the foreign clone receives standard USDC and exposes only the reviewed code, with no separate control or rescue path.",
